@node CMacs Transcribe
@chapter CMacs Transcribe: Batch Speech-to-Text

@cindex transcribe
@cindex speech-to-text
@cindex whisper
@cindex transcription
@cindex subtitles

@code{cmacs-transcribe} is a native batch @dfn{speech-to-text} subsystem and a
sibling of @code{cmacs-transcode} (@pxref{CMacs Transcode}), whose queue UI and
bounded parallel pool it mirrors.  For each queued audio or video file it runs a
three-stage pipeline entirely from inside Emacs, streaming live per-job status
into a dedicated queue buffer that redraws on a timer:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{converting} --- ffmpeg (via @code{cmacs-transcode}'s backend: a
podman/docker @code{linuxserver/ffmpeg} container, else a host @command{ffmpeg})
transcodes the input to a @emph{transient 16 kHz mono S16LE WAV}.  This is
mandatory: the embedded whisper reader accepts only that exact PCM WAV --- it
cannot decode mp3/ogg/m4a/mp4 itself the way the standalone @command{whisper-cli}
can.  The temporary WAV is deleted after transcription.
@item
@strong{transcribing} --- @code{cmacs-whisper} (whisper.cpp) turns the WAV into
text plus timestamped segments; the transcript is shown in the buffer and saved
to @file{<input>.txt}.
@item
@strong{summarizing} (optional) --- when @code{cmacs-ai} is built and enabled, an
asynchronous AI call summarizes the transcript into an Org document whose
@emph{final section is the full transcript}.
@end enumerate

It is a @emph{pure-Elisp subsystem} (no C of its own).  Enable it with
@option{--with-cmacs-transcribe} (part of the standard flag set); at runtime it
needs @option{--with-cmacs-whisper} plus podman/docker or a host
@command{ffmpeg} (and @option{--with-cmacs-ai} for summaries).  The build flag is
exposed as @code{IS-CMACS-TRANSCRIBE} and @code{(cmacs-feature-p 'transcribe)}.

