@node Transcribe Quick Start
@section Quick Start

@table @kbd
@item M-x cmacs-transcribe
In Dired this queues the marked files; elsewhere it prompts for a file or
directory (empty to add later).  Then press @key{RET}.
@end table

In the resulting @code{*cmacs-transcribe*} buffer, add more files with @kbd{a},
tune options with the single-key commands, then @key{RET} to start.  With the
shipped defaults each file yields a sidecar @file{<input>.txt} (so
@file{talk.mp4} @result{} @file{talk.mp4.txt}).  Whisper needs a model file; if
none is present @key{RET} offers to download one
(@code{cmacs-whisper-download-model}).

@node Transcribe Queue Buffer
@section The Queue Buffer

The @code{*cmacs-transcribe*} buffer (major mode @code{cmacs-transcribe-mode},
derived from @code{special-mode}) is both control panel and live status view.
Its header shows the resolved backend and current options; each queued file is
one line with a stage glyph (@code{@dots{}} queued, @code{⇄} converting,
@code{✎} transcribing, @code{✦} summarizing, @code{✔} done, @code{✗} failed,
@code{–} skipped), the file name, and --- while active --- the latest progress
detail.  A timer (@code{cmacs-transcribe-poll-interval}, default 2 s) redraws
while jobs run, and the buffer also redraws on every state change.

@subsection Jobs versus threads

Two knobs control concurrency and they are independent:

@table @kbd
@item P / p
Set @emph{jobs} (@code{cmacs-transcribe-parallel-jobs}): how many files are in
the pipeline at once.
@item w
Set @emph{threads} (@code{cmacs-transcribe-threads}, default 4): how many CPU
cores whisper.cpp uses for a @emph{single} transcription.
@end table

whisper.cpp already uses several threads per inference and serialises
transcriptions that share a model, so raising @emph{jobs} mainly parallelises the
ffmpeg conversion stage and pipelines conversion against transcription.  The sane
default is 1 job x 4 threads.

